\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Benchmark scores drive model selection, resource allocation, and research priorities for large language models, yet training data contamination is pervasive: trillion-token web crawls routinely ingest benchmark items \citep{oren2024proving}, and practitioners cannot determine how much of a reported score reflects genuine capability versus memorization \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu,chang2025benchmarking}. The community has responded with twenty mitigation strategies \citep{sun2025emperor}, contamination-free benchmarks \citep{white2024livebench}, and dynamic regeneration \citep{dong_2024_cdd}, yet a fundamental question remains unanswered: what is the actual effect of contamination on aggregate scores? The implicit assumption is that contamination inflates performance, but this has never been tested systematically across model families.

Detection methods such as Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp}, ConStat \citep{dekoninck2024constat}, and Kernel Divergence Signatures \citep{choi_2025_kds} can flag contaminated items but stop at binary classification. They do not quantify how much scores are affected or produce adjusted scores with uncertainty estimates. IRT-based evaluation frameworks \citep{polo_2024_tinybenchmarks,habba_2025_growing_pains} apply psychometric ability estimation to improve evaluation efficiency, but treat all items as equally trustworthy and ignore contamination entirely \citep{lalor_2024_irt_nlp}. \citet{han2026quantifying} apply psychometric DIF to quantify contamination effects on item parameters; our work differs by constructing a controlled testbed with known ground-truth labels across multiple families, deriving identifiability thresholds, and characterizing signal failure modes. No existing method provides an end-to-end framework for measuring contamination effect size with uncertainty quantification on existing benchmarks.

This paper systematically profiles how SFT-injected contamination affects benchmark scores across model families. Through controlled injection (LoRA, five dosage levels) into five models spanning four independent families from four labs (Alibaba, Microsoft, Allen AI, Google), we discover that contamination does not produce a single effect. Instead, five distinct dose-response patterns emerge: from immunity (Phi-4 14.7B, $\pm$0.5pp on MMLU) to catastrophic U-type collapse (Qwen-14B, $-$10pp). A baseline ability threshold at approximately 64\% MMLU separates models that benefit from contamination (OLMo-2 $+$3.2pp, Gemma-2 $+$3.8pp) from those that are harmed (Qwen-14B $-$10pp, Qwen-7B $-$3pp) or immune (Phi-4). The 14B model is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B, contradicting the assumption that scale confers robustness. We use ContamCal, a two-stage pipeline combining multi-signal item filtering with contamination-augmented IRT (2PL+$\gamma$), to provide the statistical machinery for this characterization: Stage~1 fuses detection signals via training-free Reciprocal Rank Fusion \citep{cormack_2009_rrf}; Stage~2 embeds exposure scores as covariates in an IRT model that jointly estimates ability $\theta$ and domain-level contamination effects $\gamma_d$.

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Five-pattern contamination taxonomy.} Controlled SFT injection across five models from four independent families reveals five dose-response patterns (Immune, U-collapse, V-recovery, Benefit, Benefit-saturation), governed by a baseline ability threshold at ${\sim}$64\% MMLU. These patterns are directionally consistent across MMLU and ARC-Challenge. Within the Qwen family, the 14B model is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B, directly contradicting scale-robustness assumptions. Within-family domain analysis reveals additional heterogeneity (Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$, surviving Bonferroni correction).
    \item \textbf{Signal failure characterization and enabling analysis.} Min-K\%++ exhibits directional reversal at the 0.5B scale (Cohen's $d = -1.55$ to $-1.98$), where the signal is actively misleading. RRF-guided item filtering is a prerequisite for IRT convergence: single-signal selection causes convergence failure ($\hat{R} = 1.53$), while RRF fusion preserves broader distributions necessary for $\gamma$ identifiability ($\Delta\tau = 0.18$ vs.\ size-matched random).
\end{enumerate}
Additionally, simulation studies establish that reliable $\gamma$ recovery requires $J \geq 50$ at signal AUC $\geq 0.55$ (CI width $\propto J^{-0.48}$), above current practice ($J = 8$--$30$). Current results use single-seed data; triple-seed validation is in progress.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

We systematically profiled how SFT-injected contamination affects benchmark scores across model families and scales. Three findings emerge.

\paragraph{Five patterns, not one.}
The ``contamination inflates scores'' assumption is a gross oversimplification. Controlled injection across five models reveals five distinct dose-response patterns, from immunity to catastrophic collapse, governed by a baseline ability threshold at ${\sim}$64\% MMLU. Models that lack the tested knowledge benefit from contamination (OLMo-2 $+$3.2pp, Gemma-2 $+$3.8pp); models that already possess it are harmed (Qwen-14B $-$10pp) or immune (Phi-4). These patterns are directionally consistent across MMLU and ARC-Challenge, suggesting they reflect model-level properties rather than benchmark artifacts. Any calibration or mitigation strategy that assumes a uniform contamination direction will overcorrect for some models and undercorrect for others.

\paragraph{Why bigger $\neq$ more robust.}
The Qwen-14B model ($-$10pp peak) is 3$\times$ more fragile than Qwen-7B ($-$3pp), directly contradicting scale-robustness expectations. One hypothesis: SFT injection at higher-capability models disrupts more specialized internal representations (factual associations, reasoning chains) that smaller models have not yet developed. The 14B model has more to lose from the distributional shift introduced by contaminated fine-tuning data. This has practical implications: organizations fine-tuning frontier models on mixed-quality data face greater risk of capability degradation than those fine-tuning smaller models.

\paragraph{Diagnostic value beyond aggregate calibration.}
Within-family domain analysis (Qwen2.5 $\leq$3B) reveals additional heterogeneity: Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$, while aggregate $\gamma \approx 0$ masks opposing domain-level effects. The IRT framework captures both the family-level taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}) and domain-level decomposition (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}), providing a richer characterization than any single aggregate metric. B6-DCR's overcorrection (MAE 0.108) illustrates the risk of assuming large uniform effects without measurement-theoretic grounding. Pretraining contamination may produce qualitatively different effects \citep{schaeffer2026causally}; scaling to 70B+ and testing naturally contaminated models are natural next steps.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

We identify eight limitations that bound the scope of our conclusions.

\paragraph{Scale ceiling.}
All experiments use models at or below 14.7B parameters. Larger models (70B+) may exhibit qualitatively different contamination dynamics where SFT injection is absorbed without producing measurable signal divergence.

\paragraph{Single seed.}
The five-pattern taxonomy (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}) is based on seed$=$42 data. Triple-seed validation (s123, s0) is in progress to assess pattern stability with error bars.

\paragraph{SFT ecological validity.}
We use LoRA-based SFT injection exclusively in AUGMENT mode (contaminated items appended to, not replacing, base SFT data). Pretraining-stage leakage, which occurs during large-scale web crawl ingestion, may produce qualitatively different contamination signatures. The position bias at 0.5B (A-option rate escalating from 46\% to 98\% with dosage) is likely SFT-specific. Generalization to pretraining contamination requires further validation.

\paragraph{Signal coverage.}
S1 (embedding similarity) requires training corpus access, unavailable for closed-source models. For API-only models, only S2 and S4 are applicable, and both show poor discrimination below 1.5B (\S\ref{sec:signal_failure}).

\paragraph{Ensemble size and per-domain power.}
Reliable $\gamma$ recovery requires $J \geq 50$. Our domain-level analysis ($J = 13$--$16$) yields a strong directional signal for only one of eight MMLU domains. Per-domain $\gamma$ diagnosis at current ensemble sizes is exploratory.

\paragraph{Position bias.}
Qwen2.5-0.5B exhibits position bias correlated with dosage (A-option rate: 46\% at d000 to 98\% at d100); these models are excluded from MC IRT analyses, reducing ensemble size.

\paragraph{Model misfit in non-STEM domains.}
A single linear $\gamma$ cannot capture non-monotone contamination effects within a domain. The five-pattern taxonomy operates at the model level; richer within-domain specifications may be required for domain-level calibration.

\paragraph{Additive $\gamma$ linearity.}
The 2PL+$\gamma$ model assumes contamination effects are additive and linear in exposure. PPP posterior predictive checks cannot detect threshold-type model misspecification (PPP remains 0.50--0.56 despite up to 33\% $\gamma$ overestimation), which highlights the value of simulation-based specification testing. The threshold DGP simulation uses uniform exposure; real RRF exposure is right-skewed (mean$=$0.041), so the quantitative overestimation magnitude may differ, but the qualitative direction (overestimation, not attenuation) is robust.

